<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Argonauts</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Argonauts were the band of heroes in Greek mythology who sailed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a> on the ship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argo</a> to Colchis, on the eastern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, to fetch the Golden Fleece. With the help of the Colchian princess Medea they took the fleece from King Aeetes. The story, told most fully in the Argonautica of Apollonius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, shaped Greek ideas of Colchis as a land of gold.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/argonauts.jpg" alt="Argonauts" width="330" height="280" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Niobid Painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krater_Niobid_Painter_A_Louvre_G341.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In Greek myth the Argonauts sailed in the years before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>, placed around 1300 BCE, and took their name from their ship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argo</a>, named after its builder Argus. Their quest began at Iolcus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelias">Pelias</a> had usurped the throne of his half-brother Aeson. An oracle warned Pelias against a man with one shoe, and when the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a> arrived with a single sandal, Pelias sent him to fetch the Golden Fleece. The fleece hung in a grove of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a> in Colchis, guarded by a dragon that never slept. No definite list of the crew exists. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a> names or implies Jason, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>, Castor, Polydeuces, Orpheus, Zetes, Calais and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mopsus">Mopsus</a> among others,.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The voyage included a stay on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a> and Hypsipyle had sons, and a night battle in which Jason killed King Cyzicus by mistake. Hylas was taken by a spring nymph in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysia">Mysia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> was left behind. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> the blind seer Phineus told them the way in return for freeing him from the Harpies, and advised them to send a dove through the Symplegades, the clashing rocks. They passed, and the rocks never moved again.</p><p>At Colchis King Aeëtes set <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a> tasks: yoking two fire-breathing bulls and sowing dragon's teeth from which armed men rose. Medea, his daughter, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a> caused to love Jason, gave him a drug that protected him from fire and iron, and after he swore to marry her she lulled the dragon to sleep so that he could take the fleece. They fled by night. Medea killed her brother Apsyrtus, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> sent a storm until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circe">Circe</a> purified the crew. They passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirens">Sirens</a>, whom Orpheus countered with song, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talos">Talos</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, and reached Iolcus after about four months.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Ancient writers gave other routes home. Sozomen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zosimus">Zosimus</a> say the Argonauts founded the city of Emona on the way, part of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Ljubljana</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> reports that some writers say the ship was carried across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> on men's shoulders. Porto Ferraio on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a> was known in antiquity as the portus Argous, because the Argonauts were believed to have landed there while seeking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circe">Circe</a>. The Slovenian town of Vrhnika shows a ship on its coat of arms, as it is believed to have been the starting point of the final journey on foot of the Argonauts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Argonauts” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Greeks Colchis was Aea, from the 5th century BCE onwards the home of Aeëtes, Medea and the Golden Fleece, the goal of Jason and the <b>Argonauts</b>; here Prometheus was chained to a mountain for revealing fire to humanity, and here some placed the homeland of the Amazons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-colchis">Kingdom of Colchis · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
